(5042393, 735309, 97166, '<td valign="top">Kornak watched as the sun went down, causing one of the more beautiful sunsets he could remember to flash across the sky.  A single tear streaked down his muddy, wind-burned face as he realized that it was probably the last he''d ever see. <br/> <br/>He hadn''t meant to anger the family.  He hadn''t meant for the money to disappear, but it had, and they had taken their revenge on him in this horrific way. Buried to his neck in the stinking, freezing mud of the Chantarak Shore, Kornak could only reflect on what went wrong.  He knew his time was short, for when the sun finally set, and the colors bled from the sky, they would come for him. They would be hungry.  They would feed.<br/> <br/>He watched as the sunset faded, and the tears fell faster.  At least he was going to die with this beauty as a final vision.  At least there was that.<br/> <br/>The colors bled. Darkness fell.<br/> <br/>They came.<br/> <br/>They fed.</td>', 'Kornak watched as the sun went down, causing one of the more beautiful sunsets he could remember to flash across the sky.  A single tear streaked down his muddy, wind-burned face as he realized that it was probably the last he''d ever see.  He hadn''t meant to anger the family.  He hadn''t meant for the money to disappear, but it had, and they had taken their revenge on him in this horrific way. Buried to his neck in the stinking, freezing mud of the Chantarak Shore, Kornak could only reflect on what went wrong.  He knew his time was short, for when the sun finally set, and the colors bled from the sky, they would come for him. They would be hungry.  They would feed. He watched as the sunset faded, and the tears fell faster.  At least he was going to die with this beauty as a final vision.  At least there was that. The colors bled. Darkness fell. They came. They fed.', '2008-11-03 20:59:43.000000', NULL, 1),
(5042420, 735032, 97166, '<td valign="top">The professor stopped.  He had been following the path for awhile, and it had been a pleasant stroll, but a sudden feeling of unease had come over him just now. He gazed before him, but nothing seemed amiss.  The colors of the autumn were engaging, the path dappled with leaves, the sounds of the...<br/> <br/>... that''s when it hit him. The sounds. At this time of year, there should be all kinds of noise. Birds, small mammals and insects gathering for the coming winter.  Breezes rustling the leaves.  Falling leaves and branches.  However, there was none of that.  A complete and utter silence had descended.  <br/> <br/>Again, he gazed ahead of himself on the pathway, trying to determine what this silence could mean, but he could not.  As a small bit of fear threatened to rise up in his chest, the silence was broken.  A, song, coming from further along the path. Softly at first, but then rising in volume and tempo.  A sweet, melodic lament it seemed, and he could not help but listen to its call.  Though the fear was there still, somewhere, it was quickly being buried beneath this siren song, and he found himself inexplicably drawn towards it.  Step by step, inch by inch, further and further along the path.<br/> <br/>He did not see, nor sense it, when the trees began to close in on him, surrounding him.  The air became thick, and suffocating, but he did not feel it.  The song was everything. It penetrated him, guided him.<br/> <br/>In the end, the music became him, and he became the music.  As the forest claimed his body, the music became his only, and final, thoughts. The music claimed his soul.</td>', 'The professor stopped.  He had been following the path for awhile, and it had been a pleasant stroll, but a sudden feeling of unease had come over him just now. He gazed before him, but nothing seemed amiss.  The colors of the autumn were engaging, the path dappled with leaves, the sounds of the... ... that''s when it hit him. The sounds. At this time of year, there should be all kinds of noise. Birds, small mammals and insects gathering for the coming winter.  Breezes rustling the leaves.  Falling leaves and branches.  However, there was none of that.  A complete and utter silence had descended.   Again, he gazed ahead of himself on the pathway, trying to determine what this silence could mean, but he could not.  As a small bit of fear threatened to rise up in his chest, the silence was broken.  A, song, coming from further along the path. Softly at first, but then rising in volume and tempo.  A sweet, melodic lament it seemed, and he could not help but listen to its call.  Though the fear was there still, somewhere, it was quickly being buried beneath this siren song, and he found himself inexplicably drawn towards it.  Step by step, inch by inch, further and further along the path. He did not see, nor sense it, when the trees began to close in on him, surrounding him.  The air became thick, and suffocating, but he did not feel it.  The song was everything. It penetrated him, guided him. In the end, the music became him, and he became the music.  As the forest claimed his body, the music became his only, and final, thoughts. The music claimed his soul.', '2008-11-03 21:12:58.000000', NULL, 1),
(5042423, 728666, 97166, '<td valign="top">In the final test of powers, Matchhead shocked and awed the underworld when he destroyed Pinhead by doing what was considered impossible.  He licked the blue out of the flame from a candle.<br/> <br/>It was a momentous day.</td>', 'In the final test of powers, Matchhead shocked and awed the underworld when he destroyed Pinhead by doing what was considered impossible.  He licked the blue out of the flame from a candle. It was a momentous day.', '2008-11-03 21:14:34.000000', NULL, 1),
(5042430, 735332, 97166, '<td valign="top">After a long fought battle, it was the Shadow People that raised the trophy above their head.  They had claimed the victory, and the 2008 Baja Rally was finally theirs.</td>', 'After a long fought battle, it was the Shadow People that raised the trophy above their head.  They had claimed the victory, and the 2008 Baja Rally was finally theirs.', '2008-11-03 21:15:55.000000', NULL, 1),
(5042436, 734268, 97166, '<td valign="top">Chuck and Dave always were a couple of strange and unique individuals, but on the day they each consumed a couple of cases of Rockstar Energy Drinks and then showed off just how fast they could beat their wings to win the heart of Cathy, well, it was a day that went on to infamy.</td>', 'Chuck and Dave always were a couple of strange and unique individuals, but on the day they each consumed a couple of cases of Rockstar Energy Drinks and then showed off just how fast they could beat their wings to win the heart of Cathy, well, it was a day that went on to infamy.', '2008-11-03 21:17:27.000000', NULL, 1),
(5042444, 735278, 97166, '<td valign="top">"Darrell.  Darrell.  Where are you Darrell?"  The disembodied and ghostly alien face glared down at the bench where Darrell was supposed to be, and frowned.  This was the 3rd time this week.   How could they take over the earth if their main source of intelligence kept missing his rendevous?  Where was he?  If he was playing hockey again...<br/> <br/>He waited fifteen minutes, and then, with a scowl, he dispersed back into the ether, knowing that when he finally got a hold of Darrell, there would be hell to pay.</td>', '"Darrell.  Darrell.  Where are you Darrell?"  The disembodied and ghostly alien face glared down at the bench where Darrell was supposed to be, and frowned.  This was the 3rd time this week.   How could they take over the earth if their main source of intelligence kept missing his rendevous?  Where was he?  If he was playing hockey again... He waited fifteen minutes, and then, with a scowl, he dispersed back into the ether, knowing that when he finally got a hold of Darrell, there would be hell to pay.', '2008-11-03 21:23:32.000000', NULL, 1),
(5042446, 728813, 97166, '<td valign="top">He chatted with her for what seemed like an hour.  She said all the right things. She moved like him. She smiled when he did. She flapped her wings daintily at him when he flapped at her.  He was in love, and when he bent down shyly for a kiss, she bent towards him too.<br/> <br/>When it was over, and he was flying home, one thought was on his mind.  He hadn''t expected it to be so...<br/> <br/>Wet.</td>', 'He chatted with her for what seemed like an hour.  She said all the right things. She moved like him. She smiled when he did. She flapped her wings daintily at him when he flapped at her.  He was in love, and when he bent down shyly for a kiss, she bent towards him too. When it was over, and he was flying home, one thought was on his mind.  He hadn''t expected it to be so... Wet.', '2008-11-03 21:25:45.000000', NULL, 1);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(5042450, 734942, 97166, '<td valign="top">Gerald sat on the rooftop and danced a happy dance.  He called out in glee. He yelled to the world his happiness.<br/> <br/>She had accepted!  She was to be his bride!  <br/> <br/>Another dance, another call of pure joy, and then off he flew.  It was the best day of his life.</td>', 'Gerald sat on the rooftop and danced a happy dance.  He called out in glee. He yelled to the world his happiness. She had accepted!  She was to be his bride!   Another dance, another call of pure joy, and then off he flew.  It was the best day of his life.', '2008-11-03 21:27:49.000000', NULL, 1),
(5042474, 732260, 97166, '<td valign="top">Brittany sighed.  The day had started out so well.  She''d woken up and learned how to get her diaper off by herself.  Painting was fun!  Mom hadn''t been very happy, but had laughed anyway.  Then she had a yummy breakfast and found out how great Skippy had looked with oatmeal in his fur. What a blast that was!  Then Tyler came over for a play-date and they discovered the joys of toilet paper being flushed down the toilet.  Round and round! She almost smiled again just thinking about it.  Then they''d gone to the store and when her mom turned her back, she discovered this wonderful thing called "glue".  <br/> <br/>Now though, as she sat on a seat in the corner, she sighed and wondered what had gone wrong. She wondered how SHE was supposed to know that crayons and the furnace vents didn''t mix.  She watched as the strange men in blue outfits handed Daddy a yellow piece of paper and sighed again.  Some days it just didn''t pay to be a kid.</td>', 'Brittany sighed.  The day had started out so well.  She''d woken up and learned how to get her diaper off by herself.  Painting was fun!  Mom hadn''t been very happy, but had laughed anyway.  Then she had a yummy breakfast and found out how great Skippy had looked with oatmeal in his fur. What a blast that was!  Then Tyler came over for a play-date and they discovered the joys of toilet paper being flushed down the toilet.  Round and round! She almost smiled again just thinking about it.  Then they''d gone to the store and when her mom turned her back, she discovered this wonderful thing called "glue".   Now though, as she sat on a seat in the corner, she sighed and wondered what had gone wrong. She wondered how SHE was supposed to know that crayons and the furnace vents didn''t mix.  She watched as the strange men in blue outfits handed Daddy a yellow piece of paper and sighed again.  Some days it just didn''t pay to be a kid.', '2008-11-03 21:35:48.000000', NULL, 1),
(5042566, 734942, 85334, '<td valign="top">composition is not great. <br/>the subject seems to be a bit too centered, I would have liked to see more space in front of the bird. there is a very harsh line that cuts the picture in half and does not add to a composition. taking the picture from a lower angle thus having more sky might have been an option, or alternatively, taking picture from a higher point you could have eliminated most of the sky, making the background more pleasing. Using larger apertures (if it is an option) the background could have been blurred, which makes it more pleasing as well.</td>', 'composition is not great. the subject seems to be a bit too centered, I would have liked to see more space in front of the bird. there is a very harsh line that cuts the picture in half and does not add to a composition. taking the picture from a lower angle thus having more sky might have been an option, or alternatively, taking picture from a higher point you could have eliminated most of the sky, making the background more pleasing. Using larger apertures (if it is an option) the background could have been blurred, which makes it more pleasing as well.', '2008-11-03 22:21:21.000000', NULL, 1),
